INT. DROWNED CATHEDRAL - BEEKEEPERS' LIBRARY - DUSK

Knee-deep green water fills the nave. Shelves of wax-sealed books rise like reefs from it, and bees drift between the pillars in slow gold ribbons.

TAMSIN HALE (20s, barefoot, hair tied with twine) wades along a shelf, tracing spines with two fingers. Behind her, old BRAM (70s, white beard stained with honey) skims comb into a bucket. Beside the font, twin girls PIP and POLLY (10, identical, freckled) float paper boats made of recipe pages.

TAMSIN
This one hums.

BRAM
They all hum. Leave it be.

POLLY
Pip says the roof is thinking again.

PIP
I said it was dreaming.

High above, a pane of old glass lets in the last amber light. The bees rise toward it, then settle.

A figure pushes through the west door, shaking water from a long coat of feathers: SERAPH VANE (40s, tall, ink on her lips, a lantern with no flame). She stops, hearing the hum. Smiles faintly.

SERAPH
Is the keeper in?

BRAM
The keeper is always in. He's just slow.

Tamsin slides the humming book back into its place. The bees go quiet.
